Table~\ref{tab:rank} separates the conditions by an order of magnitude. The mean
rank column states the collapse plainly: with real names the gold tail sits at
rank 1.72 of 50; with names permuted it falls to 15.65, anonymised to 23.05, and
with type tags added to the anonymised prompt to 26.71, beyond the 25.5 that
random ordering produces. Condition C fails to help; whether it actively harms
is not resolved at this sample size (see below).

\subsection{Decomposition}

Because S preserves the vocabulary and destroys only the binding between a name
and its entity, the conditions partition above-chance skill into three additive
terms. Writing $m(\cdot)$ for MRR and $c$ for chance,
\begin{equation}
m(A)-c = \underbrace{[m(A)-m(S)]}_{\text{binding}}
       + \underbrace{[m(S)-m(B)]}_{\text{readability}}
       + \underbrace{[m(B)-c]}_{\text{residual}} .
\end{equation}

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Decomposition of above-chance ranking skill on YAGO3-10. The three
terms sum to 100\,\% by construction; how the mass distributes across them does
not.}
\label{tab:decomp}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{$\Delta$MRR} & \textbf{Share} \\
\midrule
total above chance, $m(A)-c$        & 0.7269 & 100.0\,\% \\
\midrule
names permuted, $A\!\to\!S$         & 0.5195 & \textbf{71.5\,\%} \\
names removed, $S\!\to\!B$          & 0.1638 & 22.5\,\% \\
residual, $m(B)-c$                  & 0.0436 & \textbf{6.0\,\%} \\
\midrule
memorisation share                  & 0.6833 & \textbf{94.0\,\%} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

